    "rust-tokio-runtime-learn": {
      "id": "rust-tokio-runtime-learn",
      "title": "Learn: the runtime is the executor",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "direction": "guided",
      "competencyIds": [
        "rust-tokio-runtime"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain why a future does nothing until something polls it, and name the thing that polls it.",
        "Predict which values a spawned task has to own, and what the compiler refuses when it does not.",
        "Trace a task from the spawn call to the handle that carries its result."
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Ask what is running the future between the call and the await. If the terminal says cargo is not found, run the environment check first.",
        "A spawned task may outlive the function that made it, so everything it names has to be owned by it."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a spawned task runs on the thread that spawned it, when the runtime may move it to another worker thread.",
        "Believing the static bound means the value lives forever, when it means the value does not borrow from a frame that ends."
      ],
      "observed": [
        {
          "kind": "terminal",
          "command": "cargo test -p ledger-api --test runtime",
          "expect": [
            "test result",
            "running \\d+ tests"
          ],
          "shows": "test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "An async fn returns a future, and the future does nothing until somebody polls it. A runtime is the thing that polls: it holds the task list, wakes the tasks that are ready, and gives each one a slice of time."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Tokio is that runtime, and it is also a library of the async versions of the standard library. The runtime is a value you can build, so a test can drive a future on a runtime of its own without a server anywhere."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A runtime of your own, driving one future to the end:",
          "code": "let runtime = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()\n    .enable_all()\n    .build()\n    .expect(\"a runtime builds\");\nlet answer = runtime.block_on(async { 1 + 1 });"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A task is the unit the scheduler moves around. Spawning one hands the runtime a future to own, and hands you back a handle. The handle is a future too, so awaiting it is how you learn what the task produced."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A task that owns its data, and the handle that carries the answer:",
          "code": "let handle = tokio::spawn(async move {\n    values.iter().sum::<i64>()\n});\nlet total = handle.await.expect(\"the task did not panic\");"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Two bounds make that safe. The task has to be Send, because the runtime may move it between worker threads while it waits. And it has to be static, because the runtime does not know when the task will finish, so the task may not borrow from a frame that ends first."
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "What does the static bound on a spawned task stop it from doing?",
          "items": [
            "Borrowing a value the caller still owns",
            "Running on more than one worker thread",
            "Returning a value to the caller",
            "Being cancelled when the runtime shuts down"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "A spawned task may outlive the function that made it, so it cannot borrow from a frame that ends first. The Send bound is a separate rule, and it is about moving the task between worker threads."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A future does nothing until it is polled.",
            "The runtime is the thing that polls.",
            "A spawned task owns what it names.",
            "A handle is joined by awaiting it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "crates/ledger-api/src/runtime.rs is where this phase works.",
          "code": "pub fn run_on_own_runtime<F>(future: F) -> F::Output\nwhere\n    F: Future,\n{\n    todo!(\"build a runtime and drive the future on it\")\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the suite:",
          "code": "cargo test -p ledger-api --test runtime"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open runtime.rs. Build the runtime, spawn the task that owns the values, and join the handles. Run the suite and read which test failed before you change the bounds."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "This phase follows the Tokio tutorial, the Spawning and Async in depth chapters, which the Tokio project publishes under the MIT licence. The wording here is ours, and so is the ledger."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "event-flow",
        "spec": {
          "steps": [
            "a future is built",
            "the runtime polls it",
            "the task waits on an await",
            "the waker says it is ready",
            "the handle carries the result"
          ]
        },
        "caption": "Nothing happens until the runtime polls. The task waits on the await, the waker says when it is ready, and the result comes back through the handle."
      },
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Tokio tutorial: Spawning",
          "url": "https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/spawning"
        },
        {
          "label": "Tokio tutorial: Async in depth",
          "url": "https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/async"
        }
      ]
    },
    "rust-tokio-runtime-ask": {
      "id": "rust-tokio-runtime-ask",
      "title": "Reason: why a task has to own its data",
      "kind": "question",
      "mode": "review",
      "guidance": 1,
      "direction": "guided",
      "competencyIds": [
        "rust-tokio-runtime"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Ask whether the spawned task is guaranteed to finish before the spawning function returns."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking a spawned task runs to completion before spawn returns, when the handle has to be awaited for that.",
        "Believing the static bound means the value is never dropped, when it means the value does not borrow from a frame that ends."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "tokio::spawn refuses a future that borrows from the caller. Which sentence explains the static bound?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "Because a spawned task may outlive the call that spawned it.",
        "Because spawn runs the future on the current thread and forgets it.",
        "Because every future is already Send and static by default.",
        "Because a spawned task is scheduled by the runtime and may run later."
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explanation": "The task belongs to the runtime, not the stack frame that created it. Nothing forces it to finish before that frame ends, so every value it names has to live on its own, which is what the static bound asks for. Send is the other half, because the runtime may move the task to another worker thread. The other options describe guarantees the runtime does not give."
    },
