    "rust-tokio-state-learn": {
      "id": "rust-tokio-state-learn",
      "title": "Learn: one value, many tasks",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "direction": "guided",
      "competencyIds": [
        "rust-tokio-state"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what a shared handle shares and what a lock protects.",
        "Predict what happens when a lock guard is held across an await.",
        "Design a way to run a blocking piece of work that leaves the runtime free."
      ],
      "observed": [
        {
          "kind": "terminal",
          "command": "cargo test -p ledger-api --test shared",
          "expect": [
            "test result",
            "running \\d+ tests"
          ],
          "shows": "test result: ok. 3 passed; 0 failed"
        }
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Tasks share a value through a handle. The handle is cheap to clone and points at one allocation, so every task sees the same value and the last handle to go frees it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Sharing a handle is not the same as sharing the right to change it. A value that is read and written by several tasks needs a lock, and the lock is held for the update and released before anything else happens."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "A counter that two tasks can change without losing an update:",
          "code": "let counter = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0));\nlet handle = Arc::clone(&counter);\n\ntokio::spawn(async move {\n    let mut value = handle.lock().expect(\"the lock is not poisoned\");\n    *value += 1;\n});"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The guard cannot be held across an await. The guard is not a value the runtime may move between threads, so a task that holds one across an await cannot be sent, and the runtime refuses to spawn it. The rule is one line: take the lock, do the work, let the guard go."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Blocking work is the other half of the problem. A blocking call parks the thread it is on, and every other task on that thread waits for it. The runtime has a pool of threads for exactly this work."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The blocking work, moved to the pool:",
          "code": "let result = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {\n    std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(200));\n    done\n}).await;"
        },
        {
          "kind": "quickcheck",
          "body": "A task holds a lock and then waits for a channel. What goes wrong?",
          "items": [
            "The task cannot be sent between threads, so the runtime refuses it",
            "The lock is released while the task waits, so the value is unprotected",
            "The channel receives the value twice, once per thread",
            "Nothing goes wrong, because the runtime waits for the lock"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explain": "A lock guard held across an await is not a value the runtime may move between threads, so a task that holds one cannot be sent. The rule is to take the lock, do the work, and let the guard go before the next await."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "A handle shares, a lock protects.",
            "The guard must not live across an await.",
            "Blocking work belongs on the blocking pool.",
            "A short critical section is fine with the ordinary mutex."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "crates/ledger-api/src/shared.rs is where this phase works.",
          "code": "pub fn new_balances() -> Balances {\n\ttodo!(\"build the empty balances\")\n}"
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The command that runs the suite:",
          "code": "cargo test -p ledger-api --test shared"
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Open shared.rs. Share the balances, and move the blocking work off the runtime. Run the suite and read the failing check before you change the lock."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "This phase follows the Tokio tutorial, the Shared state chapter, and the Tokio guide to bridging with synchronous code, which the Tokio project publishes under the MIT licence. The wording here is ours, and so is the ledger."
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Ask what the tasks are sharing and what they are allowed to change at the same time. If the terminal says cargo is not found, run the environment check first.",
        "A blocking call parks the whole thread, so every task on that thread waits."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Thinking the shared handle copies the value, when it shares one allocation between the tasks.",
        "Believing the async mutex is always the better mutex, when it is more expensive and rarely the answer."
      ],
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Tokio tutorial: Shared state",
          "url": "https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/shared-state"
        },
        {
          "label": "Tokio: bridging with synchronous code",
          "url": "https://tokio.rs/tokio/topics/bridging"
        }
      ]
    },
